sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch07-11> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Detailed visual similarities between members of the same species, and between the two symmetric sides of a system, in features that look complex and random cannot be attributed to environmental or initial-condition randomness and must come from intrinsic randomness generation.";
    dc:type "model-of-nature";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Detailed visual similarities in organisms imply intrinsic randomness";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 7, “Mechanisms in Programs and Nature”; section “The Intrinsic Generation of Randomness” (from p. 315), p. 326. Scope: complex-looking patterns in physical, biological and other systems, especially bilaterally symmetric ones"
}
